<Plain
	title="About · today.duinlab.nl"
	description="What today.duinlab.nl is, what it keeps, where its facts come from, and its code."
>
	<Fragment slot="title">About this page</Fragment>

	<p>
		A daily page for two of us in The Hague, open to anyone: a few pictures, a few facts, a word, a quiz and a
		handful of games. It changes at midnight, Amsterdam time.
	</p>

	<h2>Privacy</h2>
	<p>
		No cookies, no analytics. Your played games, streaks, quiz answers and coffee or train choice stay in this browser's local storage
		(<code>today.games.v1</code>, <code>today.quiz.v1</code>, <code>today.mode.v1</code> for coffee or train,
		and <code>today.sunrise.v1</code> for the date of your last visit) and are never sent anywhere. Clear the site's data
		to remove them.
	</p>
	<p>
		A Cloudflare Worker serves the page. Cloudflare keeps request logs for a few days: the address asked for,
		the time, and connection details such as your IP address. I only read them to trace a fault. The day's pictures load straight from NASA, The Met, Wikimedia, the Art Institute, the Rijksmuseum or YouTube, so
		they see your IP address too.
	</p>

	<h2>Accessibility</h2>
	<p>
		The aim is WCAG 2.2 AA. Text contrast holds in every sky, games and answers are at least 44 pixels tall,
		keyboard focus is visible, motion stops when your system asks for less, and the page reads in full without
		JavaScript. CI scans the home, about and 404 pages with axe. If something gets in your way,
		<a href="https://github.com/simonvanlierde/duinlab-today/issues">open an issue</a>.
	</p>

	<h2>Sources</h2>
	<p>
		Each fact links to where it came from: Wikipedia (also its picture of the day), Wiktionary, NASA APOD, The Metropolitan Museum of Art, the Art Institute of Chicago, the Rijksmuseum,
		Open Trivia DB (CC BY-SA 4.0), ZenQuotes, and <a href="https://open-meteo.com/">Open-Meteo</a> for the
		weather. The rain line comes from <a href="https://www.buienradar.nl/">Buienradar</a>'s radar, or from
		Open-Meteo when Buienradar is down. Open-Meteo also supplies the air quality and pollen line, which shows
		only when the European AQI is 60 or more or a pollen count is high. The weather line opens
		<a href="https://www.meteoblue.com/en/weather/week/the-hague_netherlands_2747373">meteoblue</a>. The
		recipes are the newest from the Guardian, BBC Good Food and Love and Lemons. Tide times at
		Scheveningen come from <a href="https://waterinfo.rws.nl/">Rijkswaterstaat</a>, and the week's
		shows and events from <a href="https://www.paard.nl/">Paard</a>, <a href="https://www.amare.nl/">Amare</a> and <a href="https://denhaag.com/en/agenda">DenHaag.com</a>'s agendas. The sea temperature and wave height off
		Scheveningen come from <a href="https://open-meteo.com/en/docs/marine-weather-api">Open-Meteo's marine API</a>.
		The sky is worked out from the sun's position over The Hague, in your browser. The planets come from
		<a href="https://github.com/cosinekitty/astronomy">Astronomy Engine</a> (MIT), and the full moons, solstices,
		equinoxes and eclipses in the line under the date too; the meteor shower peaks are fixed approximate dates.
	</p>

	<h2>Code</h2>
	<p>
		<a href="https://github.com/simonvanlierde/duinlab-today">github.com/simonvanlierde/duinlab-today</a>, MIT
		licence. The day's data is at <a href="/today.json">today.json</a>.
	</p>
</Plain>
